{
	title: 'Plugins register useEffect style: the body sets up, its return cleans up',
	spec: 'A plugin file exports default a registration function, sync or async: (plugin) => cleanup | void. Its body does any setup and registers hooks with plugin.before, after, around and set; each hook takes effect as soon as it is registered, so code after it in the body (a repaint for a theme) already sees it. It may return a cleanup function, or a Promise resolving to one. plugin.unload and plugin.onChange do not exist; the loader never repaints by itself. Activation: on load the body runs. On reload, delete or expiry the old version is torn down first: its hooks are removed, then its cleanup runs (so a repaint there shows the old look restored); on reload the new body runs after that. A body that throws or rejects is reported loudly with the file path and error; the hooks it registered are removed, and on a reload the previous version is activated again by running its body, so the last working version stays active. A registration still pending when a newer edit, deletion or expiry arrives is superseded: once it settles its hooks are removed and its cleanup, if any, runs. A cleanup that throws is reported and teardown continues. Hooks keep the composition rules of task an (one wrapper per function, filename then registration order, original restored when the chain empties). plugins/example-plugin.ts documents this shape. Behavioral tests: hooks active during the body; cleanup runs after hook removal; a failed reload restores the previous version; a superseded async load leaks no hooks and runs its cleanup.',
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['an'],
	foldInto: ['an']
}
